Here's a script that does this, written as a reusable module with a command-line interface. Each part is marked, so you can take the checking routine out on its own.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use feature 'say';
use Readonly;

# Command line tool: perl palindrome.pl -- "a man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
# Pass --test to run the test suite instead of reading from @ARGV.
my $string = shift @ARGV;
my $check = $string ? $string : <STDIN>;
$check =~ s/\s+//g;   # remove every kind of whitespace, including newlines
$check =~ s/[^[:word:]]//g;  # keep only letters, digits and the underscore
$check = lc($check);
my $reversed = reverse($check);

my $is = $check eq $reversed ? 'is' : 'is NOT';
say "$string => ", ($is, "a palindrome." );

# --test runs these cases, or you can pass them on the command line
if (shift @ARGV // '' eq '--test' or @ARGV) {
    for my $case (@ARGV || (
        [ 'A man, a plan, a canal: Panama', 1 ],
        [ 'racecar', 1 ],
        [ 'hello', 0 ],
        [ '', 1 ],
        [ 'a', 1 ],
        [ 'ab', 0 ],
        [ 'No 'x1000', 0 ],
    )) {
        my ($input, $want) = @$case;
        $input =~ s/\s+//g;
        $input =~ s/[^[:word:]]//g;
        $input = lc($input);
        my $got = $input eq reverse($input);
        my $status = $got == $want ? 'ok' : 'FAIL';
        say "$status: ", ($want ? 'palindrome' : 'not'), " => ", $case->[0];
    }
}
```

The first thing the script does is take the string off the command line, or off the standard input if nothing was given. Then it strips whitespace and punctuation and lowers the letters, and checks whether what is left reads the same forwards and backwards. It says so in the same format for a short word and for a long one.

Here is the module itself. The interface is the same, so this is a drop-in replacement.

```perl
package Palindrome;
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use base 'Exporter';
use Scalar::Util 'looks_like_number';

our @EXPORT_OK = qw(is_palindrome normalize);

Readonly::my $NON_WORD => qr/[^[:word:]]/;

sub normalize {
    my ($string) = @_;
    die "normalize: no string given\n" unless defined $string;
    $string =~ s/\s+//g;
    $string =~ s/$NON_WORD//g;
    return lc($string);
}

sub is_palindrome {
    my ($string) = @_;
    my $normalized = normalize($string);
    return $normalized eq reverse($normalized);
}

1;
__END__

=head1 NAME

Palindrome - check whether a string reads the same forwards and backwards

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  use Palindrome qw(is_palindrome normalize);

  my $text = 'A man, a plan, a canal: Panama';
  say is_palindrome($text) ? "yes" : "no";

=head1 FUNCTIONS

=head2 normalize

Strips whitespace and punctuation from a string and lowers the letters. Leaves letters, digits and the underscore untouched.

=head2 is_palindrome

Returns true if the normalized string reads the same forwards and backwards.

=cut
```

The test suite covers the empty string, which is a palindrome in the strict sense, since nothing remains after stripping and nothing remains to contradict it. It also covers a short one, a long one, and a string of repeated letters. I chose these because most people who write this check get the empty string wrong, and I have not checked every possible input. For most real strings the strip is fast, since it touches each character once and then the comparison does the rest. For a very long string, a few hundred thousand characters or more, the reverse takes memory, and the check does not come out faster for being short.

Three things are worth knowing. The character class keeps the underscore, so `foo_bar` is checked as one word. Most people who write this check would rather it were split into `foo` and `bar` and checked separately, and the usual way to do that is to add `s/_//g;` after the strip. The other two are case and language. Lowercasing decides the case, so `MaPa` passes, which is what you asked for and might not be what you wanted. And the class is Unicode, so it keeps letters from every language it knows about, Cyrillic, Greek, Chinese characters included, and a string like `Алла` passes in Russian and `alla` passes in Swedish. The script does not decide which language a string is in, and it does not need to.

The check also does not ask about the order of the words. It reads the whole thing backwards, so `the cat sat` fails, and so does `cat the sat` if you stripped the spaces and checked `catthesat`. If your use is phrases, where word order matters more than the letters, tell me, and the version is different.

To run it: `perl palindrome.pl -- "your string here"`. The `--` keeps a string that starts with a dash from being taken as an option.

Here's the implementation. I've written it as a class with the matching logic separated out, so the program is easy to test and easy to change.

```java
import java.util.regex.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class LineMatcher {

    private static final String PROMPT_STRING = "Enter the text (type END on a separate line to finish):";
    private static final String PROMPT_REGEX = "Enter the regular expression:";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        try {
            System.out.print(PROMPT_STRING);
            String input = readMultilineInput(scanner);

            if (input.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("No text was entered. Nothing to match.");
                return;
            }

            System.out.print(PROMPT_REGEX);
            String regex = scanner.nextLine();

            if (regex.trim().isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("The regular expression was empty. No matches.");
                return;
            }

            Pattern pattern;
            try {
                pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);
            } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
                System.out.println("The regular expression is invalid: " + e.getMessage());
                return;
            }

            int count = countMatchingLines(pattern, input);

            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("=== Results ===");
            System.out.println("Text lines:               " + countLines(input));
            System.out.println("Lines matching pattern: " + count);
            System.out.println("Pattern:                  " + regex);

        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            System.out.println("Input ended unexpectedly.");
        } finally {
            scanner.close();
        }
    }

    private static String readMultilineInput(Scanner scanner) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
            String line = scanner.nextLine();
            if ("END".equals(line.trim())) {
                break;
            }
            sb.append(line).append('\n');
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    private static int countMatchingLines(Pattern pattern, String text) {
        int count = 0;
        String[] lines = text.split("\n", -1);
        for (String line : lines) {
            if (pattern.matcher(line).find()) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }

    private static int countLines(String text) {
        if (text.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }
        return text.split("\n", -1).length;
    }
}
```

The program reads lines from standard input until it finds END on its own line, which lets the user type in a long passage without needing a file. Then it compiles the pattern. If the pattern is bad, it prints the error message the Java compiler gives it, and that message is usually good enough. It prints no count at all in that case, because a count with no text behind it would look like a result. It also refuses to compile an empty pattern, since `Pattern.compile("")` matches every line, including every empty one, and a user who typed nothing for the pattern mostly wants to learn that it was empty.

The `-1` argument to `split` matters. Without it, `split("\n")` drops the trailing empty line of any text that ends with a newline, and that line counts as a line.

One thing is up to you. The program currently counts lines. If the user wants every match, including several on one line, change `find()` to `matches()` and count the matches inside the loop. If the user wants to know which lines matched, have the loop collect them in a list and print the list.

